.TH ACTIVE.JSON 1 "2026-08-10" "ragfarm" "ragfarm LLM life-cycle"
.SH NAME
active.json \- the ragfarm model registry: what is on disk, and what is resident
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I models/llm/active.json
.PP
Git-tracked. Written by
.BR fetch_llm (1)
and
.BR activate_llm (1).
Read by both, and by
.IR infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py .
.SH DESCRIPTION
One file answers two different questions, and keeping them apart is the whole
design:
.TP
.B downloaded[]
Every model this deployment
.B should have on disk.
Append-only in practice. Adding an entry is a claim that the checkpoint exists
and was verified; removing one makes its directory unclaimed and therefore
deletable by
.BR "fetch_llm.py --sync --yes" .
.TP
.B active[]
Which of them are
.B resident in GPU memory,
one array position per vLLM slot. The values are
.B indexes into
.BR downloaded[] ,
not names.
.PP
The distinction matters because they change on different timescales and for
different reasons. You fetch a model once; you re-bind slots several times a
day. Storing the binding as an index means a slot cannot reference a model that
was never registered.
.SH FORMAT
.RS
.nf
{
    "active": [0, 5],
    "downloaded": [
        {
            "model":    "Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B-Instruct-NVFP4",
            "repo":     "ig1/Qwen3-VL-30B-A3B-Instruct-NVFP4",
            "alias":    "qwen3-vl-30b-a3b-instruct-nvfp4",
            "preset":   "ragfarm-vision-instruct",
            "display":  "Qwen3-VL 30B-A3B Instruct NVFP4 (MoE)",
            "profile":  "vision-instruct",
            "comment":  "first model on the Spark; fast, weaker at multi-column reasoning",
            "size_gib": 17.9
        }
    ]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Indented four spaces, UTF-8, non-ASCII left as-is. Both writers re-serialise in
that shape on every save, so it stays reviewable in a diff.
.SH FIELDS
.TP
.B model
Directory name under
.IR models/llm/ ,
taken from the last path segment of
.BR repo .
This is what
.B activate_llm.py -m
matches. Unique.
.TP
.B repo
Hugging Face repo id. The download source, and what
.B --verify
re-checks against. Keeping it lets a bare registry rebuild a whole machine with
.BR "fetch_llm.py --sync" .
.TP
.B alias
The served name. vLLM advertises it on
.IR /v1/models ;
Open WebUI binds its preset to it. Unique, and
.B load-bearing
\- see
.B RENAMING
below.
.TP
.B preset
The Open WebUI preset id. Carries the system prompt, tool list and sampler.
Unique.
.TP
.B display
Human-readable label in the model picker. The only field with no machine
meaning, and the one a person actually reads at demo time \- say the size, the
quantisation, and dense or MoE.
.TP
.B profile
Which
.B MODEL_TUNING["profiles"]
entry in
.I infra/openwebui/setup_openwebui.py
supplies prompt, sampler and capabilities:
.B vision-thinking
or
.BR vision-instruct .
Also read by
.BR activate_llm (1)
to decide whether the slot gets
.BR --reasoning-parser .
Not cosmetic \- see
.BR fetch_llm (1).
.TP
.B comment
Free text. The place for what the next person needs and cannot infer.
.TP
.B size_gib
Total size recorded by
.BR fetch_llm (1)
.B after
verifying every file against the Hub.
.BR activate_llm (1)
sizes the GPU budget from this rather than from
.BR du ,
because a half-downloaded checkpoint measures small and would under-allocate the
slot. Absent on hand-placed models, which is why those are treated with
suspicion.
.SH THE ACTIVE ARRAY
Position is the slot number. A
.B null
means the slot is empty.
.PP
.RS
.nf
"active": [0, 5]      slot 0 -> downloaded[0], slot 1 -> downloaded[5]
"active": [0]         slot 0 only; single resident model
"active": [null, 5]   slot 0 empty, slot 1 occupied
"active": []          nothing resident
.fi
.RE
.PP
.BR "activate_llm.py --clear"
drops
.B trailing
nulls so a single-slot deployment reads as
.BR [0] .
It never drops a null in the middle: the array is positional, so removing
.B active[0]
would silently promote slot 1's model to slot 0 \- and to port 8080.
.SH CONSISTENCY RULES
.BR activate_llm (1)
validates on every run and
.B exits
rather than repairing. These are naming decisions; auto-fixing them would
produce a UI whose entries do not mean what their names say.
.IP \(bu 3
.BR model ,
.B alias
and
.B preset
must each be unique across
.BR downloaded[] .
.IP \(bu 3
Every value in
.B active[]
must be
.B null
or a valid index into
.BR downloaded[] .
.IP \(bu 3
The same index must not appear in two slots \- that would start two vLLM
instances on one set of weights, paying for it twice.
.PP
.BR fetch_llm (1)
enforces the same uniqueness
.I before
downloading, so a clash costs you a typo and not an afternoon.
.SH EDITING BY HAND
Prefer the tools; they keep
.IR .env.slot<N> ,
systemd and Open WebUI in step. But the file is plain JSON and git-tracked, and
two edits are legitimately manual:
.TP
.B Fixing a duplicate
The validator refuses to guess. Decide which entry keeps the name, edit, re-run
.BR "activate_llm.py --status" .
.TP
.B Retiring a model
Remove its object from
.BR downloaded[] .
Check first that no
.B active[]
index still points at it, and remember indexes
.B shift
\- every entry after the one you removed moves down by one, so fix
.B active[]
in the same edit. This is the single sharpest edge in the format.
.PP
After any hand edit, run
.B activate_llm.py --status
before anything else. It validates and prints, and changes nothing.
.SH RENAMING
Changing
.B alias
on a model that is currently bound is a two-step operation, because the alias is
what Open WebUI's preset points at. Edit the registry, then re-activate the slot
so the unit is restarted with the new
.B --served-model-name
and the presets are re-bound:
.RS
.nf
scripts/activate_llm.py -s 0 -m <same-model-dir>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Skip that and the preset names an alias nothing serves. The picker then offers
only the raw base models \- no system prompt, no tools, no grounding rules \-
and nothing looks broken. Answers are just silently ungrounded.
.SH WHY IT IS IN GIT
It is configuration, not state: it records intent for the whole deployment, and
the diff of a slot change is exactly the review you want. A machine can be
rebuilt from it with one command \-
.B fetch_llm.py --sync
\- so treat it with the same care as the compose file.
.PP
The generated
.IR .env.slot<N> " files are "
.B derived
from it and carry a do-not-edit header. If they disagree with the registry, the
registry is right and the fix is to re-activate the slot.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fetch_llm (1),
.BR activate_llm (1),
.BR env (1)
.PP
.I docs/decisions/ADR-0013
for the slots design;
.I models/llm/MODEL.md
for the per-model record the registry deliberately does not carry.
